From 5a83650e6ab008f42fd26b2114ee550c9a20d7b0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brandon Williams <turbine.bmw@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 18:36:35 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] serial: qcom-geni: release the OPP and ICC votes in system
 sleep instead of force-suspending

Since commit d0cd9c8d0fd5 ("serial: qcom-geni: add force suspend/resume
to system sleep callbacks") an open geni UART is power-collapsed for
system sleep: pm_runtime_force_suspend() turns the serial engine's
clocks and interconnect vote off in the normal suspend phase and they
stay off until pm_runtime_force_resume() in the normal resume phase.

On the Surface Pro 11 (X1E80100) the Surface Aggregator EC hangs off
such a UART (ttyHS0, b88000, 4 MHz, hardware flow control). The EC keeps
transmitting around system sleep (lid, keyboard-cover and battery
events, the D0-exit/entry handshake). Since 7.3, whenever a suspend
follows a resume closely, the link is dead afterwards: the D0-entry
notification and every later SAM request time out with -ETIMEDOUT,
surface_battery_resume() blocks for minutes in sequential timeouts and
the machine looks hard-locked (it panics in dpm_watchdog_handler() with
CONFIG_DPM_WATCHDOG). The failure reproduces within one to three "close
lid, open lid, suspend again within 30 s" cycles. 7.2 never showed it:
there the engine stayed clocked across the transition (the serial core's
shutdown -> pm_runtime_put cannot gate it while device_prepare() holds
its runtime PM reference), only the RX/TX commands were cancelled, and
hardware flow control held the EC off. With the engine unpowered the
EC's bytes are lost and its protocol state never recovers. The port has
no pinctrl sleep state, so pin muxing is not involved.

Simply dropping the forced suspend brings the link back (9/9 trigger
cycles) but keeps the CX rail from collapsing, because the baud-rate OPP
vote then stays in the RPMh sleep set (0 % CX residency measured over a
2 h sleep).

Release only the votes (OPP rate 0, interconnect bandwidth off) in the
system suspend callback and restore them on resume, keeping the engine
clocked and its pins in their default state. Like
pm_runtime_force_suspend()/pm_runtime_force_resume(), leave a closed,
runtime-suspended port alone: its votes are already released, and
restoring them on resume would leave it holding an interconnect vote
while it stays runtime suspended.

Measured on the Surface Pro 11: no link loss in 9/9 trigger cycles,
CX/AOSS/DDR residency 99.5 % over a 1.8 h sleep and ~0.2 W, the same as
the forced path and as 7.2. On a Lenovo Yoga Slim 7x (no EC on a geni
UART), Bluetooth works after resume and CX collapsed for 503 of the 511
seconds it slept.

Fixes: d0cd9c8d0fd5 ("serial: qcom-geni: add force suspend/resume to system sleep callbacks")
Signed-off-by: Brandon Williams <turbine.bmw@gmail.com>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
 drivers/tty/serial/qcom_geni_serial.c | 32 +++++++++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 28 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/tty/serial/qcom_geni_serial.c b/drivers/tty/serial/qcom_geni_serial.c
index a851b8f0ef04..7bbb594b8389 100644
--- a/drivers/tty/serial/qcom_geni_serial.c
+++ b/drivers/tty/serial/qcom_geni_serial.c
@@ -2107,12 +2107,29 @@ static int qcom_geni_serial_suspend(struct device *dev)
 
 	/*
 	 * When no_console_suspend is set the console must remain active
-	 * across system sleep, so skip the force suspend path.
+	 * across system sleep, so skip the vote release.
 	 */
 	if (!console_suspend_enabled && uart_console(uport))
 		return 0;
 
-	return pm_runtime_force_suspend(dev);
+	/* A closed port is runtime suspended and has released its votes. */
+	if (pm_runtime_status_suspended(dev))
+		return 0;
+
+	/*
+	 * The serial core cannot runtime-suspend the port here (the PM core
+	 * holds a runtime PM reference on every device for the whole system
+	 * sleep transition), so the baud-rate OPP vote and the interconnect
+	 * bandwidth vote would stay in RPMh's sleep set and keep the CX rail
+	 * from collapsing. Release the votes, but keep the serial engine
+	 * clocked and its pins in the default state: a remote peer such as
+	 * the Surface Aggregator EC on the Surface Pro 11 keeps transmitting
+	 * events across system sleep, and power-collapsing the engine with
+	 * pm_runtime_force_suspend() leaves that link dead after resume.
+	 */
+	if (port->se.has_opp)
+		dev_pm_opp_set_rate(dev, 0);
+	return geni_icc_disable(&port->se);
 }
 
 static int qcom_geni_serial_resume(struct device *dev)
@@ -2122,10 +2139,17 @@ static int qcom_geni_serial_resume(struct device *dev)
 	struct uart_port *uport = &port->uport;
 	struct qcom_geni_private_data *private_data = uport->private_data;
 
-	if (console_suspend_enabled || !uart_console(uport)) {
-		ret = pm_runtime_force_resume(dev);
+	/* Only a port whose votes were released in suspend gets them back. */
+	if ((console_suspend_enabled || !uart_console(uport)) &&
+	    !pm_runtime_status_suspended(dev)) {
+		ret = geni_icc_enable(&port->se);
 		if (ret)
 			return ret;
+		if (port->se.has_opp && port->clk_rate) {
+			ret = dev_pm_opp_set_rate(dev, port->clk_rate);
+			if (ret)
+				return ret;
+		}
 	}
 
 	ret = uart_resume_port(private_data->drv, uport);
-- 
2.56.0

